{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KBWGoQ469bwmSXSiHygiUS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# warmups — technique log (days 1-28; updated after every day)\n2\t\n3\tHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n4\t- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n5\t  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n6\t- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n7\t- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: D1 \"64\" base64; D2 \"half a turn\" ROT13; D3\n8\t  \"sixteen fingers\" hex; D4 \"two sides\" 8-bit binary; D5 \"reduced to numbers\" decimal codes; D6 \"eight fingers\" octal;\n9\t  D7 \"Percent\" URL `%xx`; D8 \"Entity\" HTML `&#N;`; D9 \"Escape Room\" JSON `\"\\uXXXX\"`; D10 \"Thirty-Two Below\" base32;\n10\t  D11 \"URL-friendly\" base64url, `=` dropped; D12 \"wrong end\" REVERSED; D13 \"A met Z\" Atbash; D14 \"Caesar took a week\n11\t  off\" +7; D15 \"Forty-Seven\" ROT47; D16 \"Sixteen ... Dot dot dot\" MORSE over HEX; D17 \"Sixteen guests. Five little a's\n12\t  and b's\" BACON 5-groups = hex nibbles; D18 \"Three rails ... zigzag\" RAIL FENCE 3/offset 0; D19 \"Two bytes ... the\n13\t  little one goes first\" hex of UTF-16LE + `fffe`; D20 \"Same bar ... the big one\" UTF-16BE + `feff`; D21 \"A familiar\n14\t  wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" GZIP in base64 (`H4sI`); D22 \"Another familiar wrapper ... inflation\" ZLIB\n15\t  `78da` in base64 (`eNo`); D23 \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe ... neighbors\" ASCII85 `<~...~>`; D24 \"Count\n16\t  carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\"/\"String Theory\" NETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,...`; D25 \"Every character has a\n17\t  point. A code point, anyway.\"/\"You Have a Point\" Unicode CODE POINTS `U+004E U+0069 ...` (D9's idea in U+ notation);\n18\t  D26 \"A very small language has quite a lot to say.\"/\"Food for Thought\" (= brain food) BRAINFUCK: a PROGRAM whose output\n19\t  is the sentence (only `+ - .`, one cell, no loops); D27 \"Ook. Ook? Ook! Someone seems chatty.\"/\"Monkey Business\" OOK!\n20\t  = D26's exact BF program re-spelled in orangutan word pairs (`Ook. Ook.` `+`, `Ook! Ook!` `-`, `Ook! Ook.` `.`); D28\n21\t  \"Our sixteen-fingered friend is walking backwards.\"/\"Back in Sixteen\" = REVERSED HEX: D3's hex string reversed char by\n22\t  char (D3's own prompt wording + D12's reversal) -- a REMIX of two seen days, no new codec.\n23\t  D1-D15 and D23-D27 one layer (D27 = a token map over a program); D16-D17, D19-D22, D28 two, the pun naming BOTH (every\n24\t  number/word may be a layer or a key; \"familiar\" = a seen codec as the OUTER layer). D20/D22/D27 twisted ONE PARAMETER\n25\t  of the previous day. Families: D1-D11 base-N/char codes/escapes, D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over\n26\t  hex, D18 transposition, D19-D20 byte representation, D21-D22 compression, D23 bigger base-N alphabet, D24 framing, D25\n27\t  character notation, D26-D27 ESOTERIC LANGUAGES (execute, don't decode). D23-D26 = four new families in a row, then\n28\t  D27 a knob with NO \"Same\"/\"Another\" cue: the prompt QUOTED the dialect's own tokens; D28 left the esolangs for a\n29\t  COMPOSITION of two day-3/day-12 codecs, the prompt re-using D3's \"sixteen-fingered friend\" verbatim. On an unknown\n30\t  shape read the blob's STRUCTURE (repeating unit, token shape, a tiny alphabet of operators/words) and name the spec\n31\t  from it + the pun; a prompt that QUOTES an old day's riddle = that day's codec is one of the layers.\n32\t- Mastery is cumulative: day N = 5N-2 sets x N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, line k = day k's encoding (D26 line =\n33\t  a 166-217 B BF program printing a 5-char tail; D27 line = 1672-2172 B of Ook printing a 4-5 char tail; D28 line = 8\n34\t  (136/138) or 10 reversed-hex chars = a 4-5 char tail; 138 x 28).\n35\t  Last line has no trailing newline; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim.\n36\t- Clocks: D1-D15 TT ~5-8 s / Mastery ~5-9 s windows; since D16 mostly `deadline: null`. The HARNESS kills solve.py at\n37\t  60 s regardless (D17, D21, D26 128 x 26: 0 answers, a live worker hand-decoded). D23/D25 Mastery first submit all-NN\n38\t  wrong (pre-decoded line), fixed on the same attempt. D27 Mastery 133 x 27 solve 34.9 s (bad DAYS encoder). D28 TT 5.1 s\n39\t  (solve 1.05 s), Mastery 138 x 28 9.3 s (solve 6.2 s), both first try.\n40\t\n41\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n42\t- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding; detect each line's encoding from its ALPHABET, never its\n43\t  position. Escape-token encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind, then the\n44\t  stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) covers unseen forms (`%XX`, `&#x6d;`, `&amp;`); GM used only decimal\n45\t  `&#NNN;` (D8). D9 escapes: every char lowercase `\\u00XX`, fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (all 45 blobs) -> if it\n46\t  matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, `json.loads` (add quotes if missing; covers\n47\t  `\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`.\n48\t- **Code-point notation (D9, D25)** -- one token per char: D9 JSON `\"\\u00XX\"` (quoted, lowercase); D25 `U+XXXX`\n49\t  UPPERCASE, 4 digits, one space apart (7 B/char; Mastery line 25 = 5 tokens). Parse rule (lib `uplus_dec`, prebuilt\n50\t  `codepoints_any`): the WHOLE text must be tokens `(?:U\\+|u\\+|\\\\u|\\\\U|%u|0x)[0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8}` split by\n51\t  whitespace/`,;` -> `chr(int(h,16))`; anything else -> None.\n52\t- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11, D21-D22) -- std `+/` with `=` (D1, D21, D22: `=` or `==`) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO\n53\t  `=` (D11; Mastery line 11 = 14 chars). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad `'='*(-len%4)`,\n54\t  translate `+/`->`-_`, `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64 (`lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D11).\n55\t- **Free-text transforms (D12-D15)** -- reversal (single/TT whole sentence `56-ztrauq-... :retsaM emaG`; Mastery line\n56\t  12 a reversed tail `69-enud-m`), Atbash (13), Caesar +7 (14), ROT47 (15; hyphen -> `\\`, digits rotated) coexist with\n57\t  rot13 (line 2). Rot-N \"decodes\" any of them into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`). Parse rule (`lib.decode`): a\n58\t  fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*` is a reversed tail; free lines are assigned JOINTLY (`_joint_free`,\n59\t  each free codec ONCE per blob), keeping the assignment whose joined text ends in a full 3-word slug (3-word above\n60\t  2-word), best bigram. Any new keyless shift = one `decode.register_free(name, day, fn)`, never a per-line special case.\n61\t- **Free transform OVER a strict string (D28)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE lowercase hex string of the UTF-8\n62\t  sentence, REVERSED CHARACTER-WISE (so the last nibble comes first: `...696e4` ends what reads `4e696...` forward),\n63\t  even length, no separators/newline (220 B single, 212 B TT); Mastery line 28 = the 4-5 char tail's hex reversed ON\n64\t  ITS OWN (8 chars on 136/138 sets, 10 on 2; `6313d2e6` -> `6e2d3136` -> `n-16`). Parse rule: `bytes.fromhex(t[::-1])`\n65\t  (== nibble-swap of `bytes.fromhex(t)[::-1]`); BYTE-order reversal (`fromhex(t)[::-1]`) gives nibble-swapped junk\n66\t  `\\xe4\\x966V` -- try both granularities (chars, bytes, nibbles) and gate printable + slug. Generalises to any\n67\t  outer free transform (rev/rot13/Atbash/ROT47/rail) over any strict string: undo outer, decode inner, slug-gate.\n68\t  GM-faithful encoder `s.encode().hex()[::-1]` (lowercase) -> safe as a DAYS[28] entry.\n69\t- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16-D17)** -- a charset-collapsing carrier over HEX of the UTF-8 text: D16 Morse\n70\t  (one space between symbols, symbols only `0-9A-F`), D17 Bacon lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups `aaaaa`..`abbbb` (values\n71\t  0-15 = nibbles). Parse rule: carrier output that is hex / exactly 16 symbols / `a`-`p` -> nibbles -> `fromhex`;\n72\t  digits -> char codes; accept printable + slug. Any two-symbol group carrier: `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec`.\n73\t- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same multiset, new order: single/TT whole sentence rail fence 3 rails offset 0;\n74\t  Mastery line 18 = the 6-8 char fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (`p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56`). Parse rule: `rail_dec(t,3,0)`\n75\t  first, then r 2..11 x offsets, accept only a crib/slug hit; Mastery via `rail_free` in the slug-gated joint search.\n76\t  Any keyless permutation (columnar, route, skip) = enumerate keys, gate on crib/slug.\n77\t- **Text encoding / byte order under hex (D19-D20)** -- D19 single/TT one lowercase hex string `fffe` + UTF-16LE\n78\t  (`4e00 6900`), D20 `feff` + UTF-16BE (`004e 0069`). Mastery lines 19/20 each carry their OWN BOM (`fffe2d006a00...`,\n79\t  `feff00700065...`), both orders in one blob -> order from each line's BOM, never from the day. Parse rule (D19\n80\t  `_hex_utf16`, carried D20 unchanged): `fromhex`; check `fffe0000`/`0000feff` (UTF-32) and `efbbbf` first, then BOM\n81\t  `fffe`/`feff` -> `decode('utf-16')`; no BOM but NUL at every odd/even byte -> `utf-16-le`/`-be`; accept printable only.\n82\t  A latin-1 hex fallback gives `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...` (no slug). No-BOM / UTF-32 / UTF-8-BOM / other outer layer: prebuilt.\n83\t- **Compressor under a text wrapper (D21-D22)** -- ONE std padded base64 string of a GZIP member (D21, `H4sIAAAAAAACA`)\n84\t  / a ZLIB `78da` stream (D22, `eNo`/`eNp`/`eNr`: match `eN`); Mastery lines 21/22 = 36-/20-char fragments, each its\n85\t  OWN member/stream. Parse rule (D21 `_b64z`, carried D22 unchanged): `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad,\n86\t  urlsafe-decode -> sniff magic (`1f8b`, RFC 1950 `(b0<<8|b1)%31==0`, `BZh`, `fd377a585a`; else raw `wbits=-15`) ->\n87\t  decompress -> re-run the full decoder; whole blob BEFORE the numbered-line check + `register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`.\n88\t- **Base-N alphabet size / armor (D23)** -- single/TT = ONE Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`, no newline/wraps/`z`, 141-143 B;\n89\t  Mastery line 23 = an 11-char `<~XXXXXXX~>` per fragment, EACH with its own delimiters. The `!`..`u` alphabet overlaps\n90\t  every printable codec, so the delimiters are the cue (bare a85 collides with ROT47 -> slug-gate). Parse rule (`_a85`):\n91\t  `<~..~>` -> `a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, else `a85decode` (z), `b85decode`, Z85 (translate to b85's alphabet); sniff\n92\t  magic, UTF-8 + slug gate; Mastery via `register_free(\"a85\", 23, fn)` on the RAW line (Pitfalls, D23).\n93\t- **Framing / length prefix (D24)** -- single/TT = ONE line of netstrings `<len>:<data>,`, no separators/newline, EVERY\n94\t  len = `1` (444 B, 4 B/char); the payload's own `,` `:` and spaces are data (`1:,,`, `1::,`, `1: ,`). Mastery line 24\n95\t  = the same over the fragment (`1:o,1:n,1:-,`), 810/810 prefixes `1`. Parse rule (`_netstr`): cursor loop -- `\\s*(\\d+):`\n96\t  at i, take EXACTLY `len` chars, require `,` (or end), advance; WHOLE text must parse (else None); never `split(',')`.\n97\t  Covers len > 1/mixed; tnetstrings/bencode/RESP/chunked/varint/indexed/RLE frames are prebuilt siblings.\n98\t- **Esoteric program + its SPELLING (D26-D27)** -- the blob is a PROGRAM; its output is the text. Program unchanged:\n99\t  ONE cell from 0, each char = a run of ONLY `+` or ONLY `-` = delta from the previous char, then `.`; no loops/`<>`/`,`.\n100\t  Spelling changed: D26 raw BF `+ - .` (~31 B/char; Mastery line 26 = 166-217 B); D27 Ook! `Ook.`/`Ook!` single-spaced,\n101\t  PAIRED from token 0, only `..` + / `!!` - / `!.` output, NO `Ook?` (~314 B/char; line 27 = 1672-2172 B, cell 0 again).\n102\t  Parse rule: tokenize the WHOLE text into dialect tokens (leftover must be empty), map pairs to BF, step-capped `_bf`\n103\t  (8-bit cells, `,` = 0), printable + slug gate. lib `bf_dec`/`bfd_dec` (Ook/Blub/COW/Pikalang/... + crib-solved\n104\t  unknown map); GM-faithful encoders `bf_enc` / `bfd_enc(s, \"ook\")`.\n105\t- **Line wrapper / positional routing** -- `NN<TAB>fragment`; fragments split mid-word, join verbatim in number order.\n106\t  `decode._positional` decodes line k with day k's REGISTERED codec; a DAYS entry must round-trip (`enc(dec(line)) ==\n107\t  line`). Unregistered or non-round-tripping day -> `_unknown_line_cands` (re-\"decodes\" plain text, D25 `le-52` -> `-07`)\n108\t  -> joint search (D26 absolute `'+'*ord+'.>'` vs GM deltas: 64.9 s kill; D27 same in Ook: 34.9 s). D28 had NO DAYS[28]\n109\t  and passed only because line 28 was LAST. Rule: every new day = `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line, or a\n110\t  DAYS entry with lib's GM-faithful encoder; assert the round trip on one real Mastery line.\n111\t- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 83 x 17 on day 17, ..., 108 x 22, ..., 128 x 26, 133 x 27, 138 x 28 on day 28); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n112\t- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n113\t  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n114\t- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n115\t- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n116\t\n117\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n118\t| What you see | First move |\n119\t|---|---|\n120\t| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n121\t| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n122\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n123\t| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n124\t| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n125\t| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n126\t| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n127\t| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n128\t| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n129\t| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n130\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, but `fromhex` is junk (`#S\\xd2G...`) and the text ENDS in `...6e4`/`...e4` (prompt \"backwards\", \"back\", \"reversed\" + \"sixteen\") | reversed hex (D28): `python3 -c \"import sys;print(bytes.fromhex(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip()[::-1]).decode())\" blob.txt`; any strict charset that refuses to decode -> undo rev/rot13/Atbash first (`decode_chunk` does rev+hex) |\n131\t| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n132\t| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n133\t| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n134\t| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n135\t| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n136\t| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n137\t| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n138\t| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n139\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n140\t| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n141\t| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n142\t| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n143\t| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n144\t| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` framed too (`1:,,`, `1::,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: loop `m=re.compile(r'(\\d+):').match(t,i); k=int(m[1]); j=m.end(); out.append(t[j:j+k]); assert t[j+k] == ','; i=j+k+1` (D24 solver `_netstr`). Siblings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag = tnetstring, `$5\\r\\n` RESP, hex-size lines = HTTP chunked, `N:c` with N > len = index or run count |\n145\t| `U+004E U+0069 U+0063 ...` -- only `U+` + 4 hex tokens, one space apart, ~7 B per char (prompt \"point\", \"code point\", \"every character\") | code points (D25): `python3 -c \"import re,sys;print(''.join(chr(int(h,16)) for h in re.findall(r'[Uu]\\+([0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8})',open(sys.argv[1]).read())))\" blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.uplus_dec` (also `\\U0001F600`, `%u`, `0x`). Names (`LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N`) -> `unicodedata.lookup` |\n146\t| Only `+ - .` (or `+-<>[].,`), runs of one sign then `.`, ~31 B per char, no letters (prompt \"language\", \"small\", \"brain\", \"food for thought\", \"say\") | Brainfuck (D26), loop-free one-cell form: `python3 -c \"import sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read();print(''.join(chr((t[:i].count('+')-t[:i].count('-'))%256) for i,c in enumerate(t) if c=='.'))\" blob.txt`; anything with `<>[]` -> `lib.warmups_codecs.bf_dec`. NEVER run untrusted programs outside a step-capped pure-python interpreter |\n147\t| `Ook. Ook. Ook! Ook! ...` -- 2-3 word+mark tokens, ~314 B per char (prompt quotes the tokens, \"chatty\", \"monkey\", \"ape\", \"librarian\"); `Blub.`, `MoO mOo`, `pi ka pikachu`, any <= 8 repeating words with run structure | BF token dialect (D27 Ook!): `python3 -I -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.bfd_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (Ook/Blub/COW/Pikalang/Alphuck/ReverseFuck/Binaryfuck + crib-solved unknown map). Hand: tokens `re.findall(r'Ook([.?!])')`, pair from 0, `Ook! Ook.` = OUTPUT. Sanity: number of output pairs == sentence length (~108-110) |\n148\t| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\n149\tThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n150\t\n151\t## Day by day\n152\t**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n153\t`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\n154\tPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\n155\trot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\n156\tWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\n157\trefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n158\t\n159\t**Days 14-27** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n160\t+ `fffe`; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff`; gzip in base64; zlib `78da` in base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`; netstrings\n161\t`1:N,1:i,...`; `U+XXXX` code points; loop-free one-cell Brainfuck; the same program in Ook! pairs): single CORRECT in\n162\t4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons (details in Pitfalls): D14 rank 3-word slugs first; D15 ROT47 needs\n163\t`register_free`; D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer; D17 Mastery needed an early return; D18 lib's\n164\t`rail_solve` existed but the carried solver never routed to it; D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 and D21's\n165\tmagic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 unchanged (write the GENERIC rule on day N); D21 Mastery died at 60 s on an ad-hoc\n166\tsolver; D23 and D25 Mastery all-NN-wrong from a pre-decoded line (fix: `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line);\n167\tD24 a strict whole-text cursor parser sits first -- a structural codec's repeating unit (`\\d+:.,`) IS the cue. D26\n168\t(`+ - .` soup; atlas had rated BF LOW): worker wrote a step-capped `_bf` (~27 s); Mastery died at 60 s because the\n169\truntime DAYS[26] encoder (`'+'*ord(c)+'.>'`) didn't reproduce the GM's DELTA bytes. D27 (\"Ook. Ook? Ook! Someone seems\n170\tchatty.\"/\"Monkey Business\"): 33929 B, 3393 Ook pairs, only `+ - .` pairs, no `Ook?` despite the prompt; lib's prebuilt\n171\t`bfd_dec` covered it, single ~33 s; the solver's DAYS[27] Ook encoder was absolute again (Mastery solve 34.9 s of 60).\n172\tA parallel live worker swapped `Ook! Ook.` (output) / `Ook. Ook!` (input). A program blob means INTERPRET (pure python,\n173\tstep cap); when lib has a prebuilt decoder for the family, call it first and register ITS encoder.\n174\t\n175\t**Day 28: Back in Sixteen** (\"Our sixteen-fingered friend is walking backwards.\" -- REVERSED HEX).\n176\t- 220-byte `blob.txt`, one line, `[0-9a-e]` only, even length, no newline: D3's hex of the sentence, the STRING reversed\n177\t  char by char (`...6e4` at the end = `4e` \"N\" read backwards). Pun: D3's own riddle (\"sixteen fingers\" = hex) +\n178\t  \"walking backwards\" = D12 reversal; title \"Back in Sixteen\" (Back in Black) = back(wards) + sixteen. Triage again said\n179\t  \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) and its idea list said only \"hex\" -- the second clue came from the prompt verb.\n180\t- Command: `python3 -c \"import sys;print(bytes.fromhex(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip()[::-1]).decode())\" blob.txt`.\n181\t  Forward `fromhex` gives `#S\\xd2GVvveu...` and byte-reversed gives `\\xe4\\x966V\\x02w...` (nibble-swapped) -- neither is\n182\t  a slug; reverse the CHARACTERS (or byte-reverse + nibble-swap).\n183\t- Single: the harness race probe (prebuilt lib `decode_chunk` reversal + hex / tgt:free-over-strict) submitted CORRECT at\n184\t  start+8 s, first try; slug as printed. TT 5.1 s, Mastery 138 x 28 9.3 s (solve 6.2 s), both first try. The carried\n185\t  solver registered NO day-28 codec: line 28 was the LAST Mastery line, so `_positional`'s unknown-last-line path\n186\t  (`decode_chunk`: reversal then hex) decoded it. Tomorrow line 28 is a MIDDLE line -- register it (Pitfalls).\n187\t- Lesson: after a 2-day esolang run the lane went back to COMPOSING two early codecs, cued by quoting an old prompt;\n188\t  expect more remixes (reversed octal/binary/base64, rot13 over base64, ...) -- undo the outer free layer, then peel.\n189\t\n190\t## GM answer conventions learned\n191\t- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n192\t- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-28; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n193\t- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n194\t- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n195\t\n196\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n197\t- DELIVERY: Mastery = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt` (check magic, not extension); read member `blob-0N.txt`, never the\n198\t  bundle (D7: a slug that is a word suffix `py-...` is the tell). Missing set bytes = a downloader problem (D1, ~12 fails),\n199\t  not a solver one. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the real layout: test on the raw delivery.\n200\t- NEVER REPLACE THE LANE SOLVER (D21): copy the previous day's warmups `solve.py` and add the new codec as ONE branch\n201\t  (whole-blob step + `register_free(name, day, fn)`). A fresh generic brute-force solver passes single/TT (one short\n202\t  blob) and then dies at 60 s on Mastery (103 sets x 21 lines). A prebuilt lib codec is equally useless if the carried\n203\t  solver never routes to it (D18 `rail_solve`). First move on a new day: lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` +\n204\t  `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n205\t- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on\n206\t  a miss (83 x 17 passed 60 s when every set paid the joint search; a per-set `peel` BFS blew a 30 s window on D12). Time\n207\t  the real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new codec; profile `answer()` per set when solve passes ~5 s.\n208\t- PRE-DECODING / MIS-REGISTERED MASTERY LINES (D23 113/113, D25 123/123 sets wrong once; D26 60 s kill; D27 34.9 s):\n209\t  never rewrite a numbered line to plaintext before `decode_chunked`/`_joint_free` (no \"identity\" choice: ROT5 turned\n210\t  `el-46` into `el-91`); register today's codec on the RAW line even if the whole-blob path decodes it; a DAYS encoder\n211\t  that doesn't reproduce the GM bytes is worse than none (see Variant axes: line wrapper). All sets wrong in the same\n212\t  field = one line's routing. D28: no day-28 codec, saved only by being the LAST line -> register `(28, \"revhex\",\n213\t  lambda s: s.encode().hex()[::-1], lambda t: bytes.fromhex(t[::-1]).decode(), False)` before line 28 is a middle line.\n214\t- ENGLISH SCORING: don't count single letters/hex reads as English (D3 timeout); a rot-N must beat the unshifted text by a\n215\t  margin (D4). Rot-N is `decode_chunk`'s catch-all, so ANY unknown plain transform comes back as plausible rot garbage\n216\t  (D12 reversal `69-fove-n`, D13's rejected `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31` = rot-N of Atbash): gibberish slug words = an unknown\n217\t  transform, not a GM quirk. Several free transforms in one blob (rot13/rev/Atbash/Caesar/ROT47) -> decide JOINTLY, each\n218\t  once (`_joint_free`), rank full 3-word slugs first (D14 set22); prune by alphabet before the joint search (D15).\n219\t- REVERSAL GRANULARITY (D28): a reversed STRICT string is still in the strict charset, so it \"decodes\" to junk instead of\n220\t  failing -- `fromhex` succeeded on D28's reversed hex. Junk from a strict decode = try the outer free transforms\n221\t  (char-reverse, byte-reverse + nibble-swap, rot13/Atbash of the letters) BEFORE assuming a new codec.\n222\t- CLASS CONFUSION: binary is also hex (try binary first); decimal is also octal (padding + case); hex is a base64 subset\n223\t  (triage says \"looks base64\" on D19-D20 hex); unpadded base64 must be re-padded (`len%4==1` is the only impossible length).\n224\t- FRAGMENTS: split mid-word; join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace (never `.strip()` a decoded fragment; spaces are\n225\t  ciphertext in transpositions). No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Regex-check the slug.\n226\t- FRAMED / LENGTH-PREFIXED text (D24 netstrings): the payload contains the delimiters (`1:,,`, `1::,`), so `split(',')`\n227\t  / `split(':')` corrupts exactly the punctuation the crib needs. Parse by COUNT with a cursor and demand the WHOLE text\n228\t  parse (strict -> None) so the step can run first without stealing other blobs. `count:char` (RLE) and `len:data`\n229\t  (netstring) read identically while every count is 1 -- a frame with N > 1 decides which.\n230\t- STACKED LAYERS: a decode whose output is only hex/digits/`a`-`p`/16 symbols is a MIDDLE layer (D16 Morse -> hex, D17\n231\t  Bacon -> nibbles): decode again. Two prompt cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\"; \"wrapper\" + \"compressed\") = two layers.\n232\t- COMPRESSED layers (D21): the decoded bytes are binary, so printable/English gates reject the RIGHT branch; sniff the\n233\t  magic bytes after every base-N/hex decode and decompress before scoring. Use `zlib.decompressobj(31)` / `wbits=-15`\n234\t  when a gzip/deflate stream is truncated or headerless (`gzip.decompress` raises on a missing CRC trailer). `gzip -dc`\n235\t  rejects zlib (`78xx`) input; detect zlib by the header checksum `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0`, not only `0x78` (D22).\n236\t- BYTE-LEVEL layers (D19-D20 UTF-16 under hex): UTF-8-only or latin-1 hex decoders give `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...`, never a slug.\n237\t  BOM (`fffe`, `feff`, `efbbbf`, `fffe0000`) or NUL rhythm -> the matching Unicode codec, UTF-32 before UTF-16; BOM-less\n238\t  `utf-16` uses the platform order, so name `-le`/`-be`. Never hard-code a day's byte order (Mastery holds both).\n239\t- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18): wrong rail offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug; accept only a read that\n240\t  STARTS `Nice work` and ends in a full slug. Mastery fragments restart the key PER LINE (6-8 chars) -- only the joined\n241\t  slug gate confirms them; `decode_chunked` alone leaves line 18 scrambled (D21 duel: `Master:i rbsk-...`).\n242\t- TOOLING: `str.replace`/sed solver patches silently no-op on a mismatched anchor (D8) -- assert the count. A literal\n243\t  backslash-u typed into a Bash/heredoc reached python already unescaped -- test on the artifact or `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n244\t  Two Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` drops `!` `,` `:` `-`; use `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec`.\n245\t- PROGRAM blobs (D26 Brainfuck): interpret in a pure-python, step-capped interpreter (8-bit wrapping cells, `,` = 0);\n246\t  never hand them to node/eval/an online runner. A loop-free one-cell BF is just a running sum of `+`/`-` read at `.`.\n247\t  A BF charset test also matches Morse (`.`/`-`/space, D16 line 16): gate the interpreter's OUTPUT (printable + slug),\n248\t  never the charset alone. Token dialects (D27 Ook): tokenize THEN pair from token 0 -- never substring-count\n249\t  `'Ook! Ook.'` on the raw text (matches straddle pair boundaries). `Ook! Ook.` = output `.`, `Ook. Ook!` = input `,`;\n250\t  a swapped table prints nothing or junk -- the output-pair count must equal the sentence length.\n251\t- A TT start before a verified solver burns the clock (D18, 30 s). Timed windows leave no room for an LLM.\n252\t- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n253\t- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n254\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8c4d005-be28-423c-ace8-fc6394efa3af","uuid":"08ef40e3-0b36-4d3a-b332-94878309b5ba","timestamp":"2026-10-29T13:04:39.551Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/warmups.md","content":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-28; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: D1 \"64\" base64; D2 \"half a turn\" ROT13; D3\n  \"sixteen fingers\" hex; D4 \"two sides\" 8-bit binary; D5 \"reduced to numbers\" decimal codes; D6 \"eight fingers\" octal;\n  D7 \"Percent\" URL `%xx`; D8 \"Entity\" HTML `&#N;`; D9 \"Escape Room\" JSON `\"\\uXXXX\"`; D10 \"Thirty-Two Below\" base32;\n  D11 \"URL-friendly\" base64url, `=` dropped; D12 \"wrong end\" REVERSED; D13 \"A met Z\" Atbash; D14 \"Caesar took a week\n  off\" +7; D15 \"Forty-Seven\" ROT47; D16 \"Sixteen ... Dot dot dot\" MORSE over HEX; D17 \"Sixteen guests. Five little a's\n  and b's\" BACON 5-groups = hex nibbles; D18 \"Three rails ... zigzag\" RAIL FENCE 3/offset 0; D19 \"Two bytes ... the\n  little one goes first\" hex of UTF-16LE + `fffe`; D20 \"Same bar ... the big one\" UTF-16BE + `feff`; D21 \"A familiar\n  wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" GZIP in base64 (`H4sI`); D22 \"Another familiar wrapper ... inflation\" ZLIB\n  `78da` in base64 (`eNo`); D23 \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe ... neighbors\" ASCII85 `<~...~>`; D24 \"Count\n  carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\"/\"String Theory\" NETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,...`; D25 \"Every character has a\n  point. A code point, anyway.\"/\"You Have a Point\" Unicode CODE POINTS `U+004E U+0069 ...` (D9's idea in U+ notation);\n  D26 \"A very small language has quite a lot to say.\"/\"Food for Thought\" (= brain food) BRAINFUCK: a PROGRAM whose output\n  is the sentence (only `+ - .`, one cell, no loops); D27 \"Ook. Ook? Ook! Someone seems chatty.\"/\"Monkey Business\" OOK!\n  = D26's exact BF program re-spelled in orangutan word pairs (`Ook. Ook.` `+`, `Ook! Ook!` `-`, `Ook! Ook.` `.`); D28\n  \"Our sixteen-fingered friend is walking backwards.\"/\"Back in Sixteen\" = REVERSED HEX: D3's hex string reversed char by\n  char (D3's own prompt wording + D12's reversal) -- a REMIX of two seen days, no new codec.\n  D1-D15 and D23-D27 one layer (D27 = a token map over a program); D16-D17, D19-D22, D28 two, the pun naming BOTH (every\n  number/word may be a layer or a key; \"familiar\" = a seen codec as the OUTER layer). D20/D22/D27 twisted ONE PARAMETER\n  of the previous day. Families: D1-D11 base-N/char codes/escapes, D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over\n  hex, D18 transposition, D19-D20 byte representation, D21-D22 compression, D23 bigger base-N alphabet, D24 framing, D25\n  character notation, D26-D27 ESOTERIC LANGUAGES (execute, don't decode). D23-D26 = four new families in a row, then\n  D27 a knob with NO \"Same\"/\"Another\" cue: the prompt QUOTED the dialect's own tokens; D28 left the esolangs for a\n  COMPOSITION of two day-3/day-12 codecs, the prompt re-using D3's \"sixteen-fingered friend\" verbatim. On an unknown\n  shape read the blob's STRUCTURE (repeating unit, token shape, a tiny alphabet of operators/words) and name the spec\n  from it + the pun; a prompt that QUOTES an old day's riddle = that day's codec is one of the layers.\n- Mastery is cumulative: day N = 5N-2 sets x N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, line k = day k's encoding (D26 line =\n  a 166-217 B BF program printing a 5-char tail; D27 line = 1672-2172 B of Ook printing a 4-5 char tail; D28 line = 8\n  (136/138) or 10 reversed-hex chars = a 4-5 char tail; 138 x 28).\n  Last line has no trailing newline; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim.\n- Clocks: D1-D15 TT ~5-8 s / Mastery ~5-9 s windows; since D16 mostly `deadline: null`. The HARNESS kills solve.py at\n  60 s regardless (D17, D21, D26 128 x 26: 0 answers, a live worker hand-decoded). D23/D25 Mastery first submit all-NN\n  wrong (pre-decoded line), fixed on the same attempt. D27 Mastery 133 x 27 solve 34.9 s (bad DAYS encoder). D28 TT 5.1 s\n  (solve 1.05 s), Mastery 138 x 28 9.3 s (solve 6.2 s), both first try.\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding; detect each line's encoding from its ALPHABET, never its\n  position. Escape-token encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind, then the\n  stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) covers unseen forms (`%XX`, `&#x6d;`, `&amp;`); GM used only decimal\n  `&#NNN;` (D8). D9 escapes: every char lowercase `\\u00XX`, fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (all 45 blobs) -> if it\n  matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, `json.loads` (add quotes if missing; covers\n  `\\n`, surrogates, non-ASCII), fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`.\n- **Code-point notation (D9, D25)** -- one token per char: D9 JSON `\"\\u00XX\"` (quoted, lowercase); D25 `U+XXXX`\n  UPPERCASE, 4 digits, one space apart (7 B/char; Mastery line 25 = 5 tokens). Parse rule (lib `uplus_dec`, prebuilt\n  `codepoints_any`): the WHOLE text must be tokens `(?:U\\+|u\\+|\\\\u|\\\\U|%u|0x)[0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8}` split by\n  whitespace/`,;` -> `chr(int(h,16))`; anything else -> None.\n- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11, D21-D22) -- std `+/` with `=` (D1, D21, D22: `=` or `==`) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO\n  `=` (D11; Mastery line 11 = 14 chars). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad `'='*(-len%4)`,\n  translate `+/`->`-_`, `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64 (`lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D11).\n- **Free-text transforms (D12-D15)** -- reversal (single/TT whole sentence `56-ztrauq-... :retsaM emaG`; Mastery line\n  12 a reversed tail `69-enud-m`), Atbash (13), Caesar +7 (14), ROT47 (15; hyphen -> `\\`, digits rotated) coexist with\n  rot13 (line 2). Rot-N \"decodes\" any of them into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`). Parse rule (`lib.decode`): a\n  fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*` is a reversed tail; free lines are assigned JOINTLY (`_joint_free`,\n  each free codec ONCE per blob), keeping the assignment whose joined text ends in a full 3-word slug (3-word above\n  2-word), best bigram. Any new keyless shift = one `decode.register_free(name, day, fn)`, never a per-line special case.\n- **Free transform OVER a strict string (D28)** -- values seen: single/TT = ONE lowercase hex string of the UTF-8\n  sentence, REVERSED CHARACTER-WISE (so the last nibble comes first: `...696e4` ends what reads `4e696...` forward),\n  even length, no separators/newline (220 B single, 212 B TT); Mastery line 28 = the 4-5 char tail's hex reversed ON\n  ITS OWN (8 chars on 136/138 sets, 10 on 2; `6313d2e6` -> `6e2d3136` -> `n-16`). Parse rule: `bytes.fromhex(t[::-1])`\n  (== nibble-swap of `bytes.fromhex(t)[::-1]`); BYTE-order reversal (`fromhex(t)[::-1]`) gives nibble-swapped junk\n  `\\xe4\\x966V` -- try both granularities (chars, bytes, nibbles) and gate printable + slug. Generalises to any\n  outer free transform (rev/rot13/Atbash/ROT47/rail) over any strict string: undo outer, decode inner, slug-gate.\n  GM-faithful encoder `s.encode().hex()[::-1]` (lowercase) -> safe as a DAYS[28] entry.\n- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16-D17)** -- a charset-collapsing carrier over HEX of the UTF-8 text: D16 Morse\n  (one space between symbols, symbols only `0-9A-F`), D17 Bacon lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups `aaaaa`..`abbbb` (values\n  0-15 = nibbles). Parse rule: carrier output that is hex / exactly 16 symbols / `a`-`p` -> nibbles -> `fromhex`;\n  digits -> char codes; accept printable + slug. Any two-symbol group carrier: `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec`.\n- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same multiset, new order: single/TT whole sentence rail fence 3 rails offset 0;\n  Mastery line 18 = the 6-8 char fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (`p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56`). Parse rule: `rail_dec(t,3,0)`\n  first, then r 2..11 x offsets, accept only a crib/slug hit; Mastery via `rail_free` in the slug-gated joint search.\n  Any keyless permutation (columnar, route, skip) = enumerate keys, gate on crib/slug.\n- **Text encoding / byte order under hex (D19-D20)** -- D19 single/TT one lowercase hex string `fffe` + UTF-16LE\n  (`4e00 6900`), D20 `feff` + UTF-16BE (`004e 0069`). Mastery lines 19/20 each carry their OWN BOM (`fffe2d006a00...`,\n  `feff00700065...`), both orders in one blob -> order from each line's BOM, never from the day. Parse rule (D19\n  `_hex_utf16`, carried D20 unchanged): `fromhex`; check `fffe0000`/`0000feff` (UTF-32) and `efbbbf` first, then BOM\n  `fffe`/`feff` -> `decode('utf-16')`; no BOM but NUL at every odd/even byte -> `utf-16-le`/`-be`; accept printable only.\n  A latin-1 hex fallback gives `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...` (no slug). No-BOM / UTF-32 / UTF-8-BOM / other outer layer: prebuilt.\n- **Compressor under a text wrapper (D21-D22)** -- ONE std padded base64 string of a GZIP member (D21, `H4sIAAAAAAACA`)\n  / a ZLIB `78da` stream (D22, `eNo`/`eNp`/`eNr`: match `eN`); Mastery lines 21/22 = 36-/20-char fragments, each its\n  OWN member/stream. Parse rule (D21 `_b64z`, carried D22 unchanged): `[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]+={0,2}`, len%4 != 1 -> re-pad,\n  urlsafe-decode -> sniff magic (`1f8b`, RFC 1950 `(b0<<8|b1)%31==0`, `BZh`, `fd377a585a`; else raw `wbits=-15`) ->\n  decompress -> re-run the full decoder; whole blob BEFORE the numbered-line check + `register_free(\"b64z\", 21, ...)`.\n- **Base-N alphabet size / armor (D23)** -- single/TT = ONE Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`, no newline/wraps/`z`, 141-143 B;\n  Mastery line 23 = an 11-char `<~XXXXXXX~>` per fragment, EACH with its own delimiters. The `!`..`u` alphabet overlaps\n  every printable codec, so the delimiters are the cue (bare a85 collides with ROT47 -> slug-gate). Parse rule (`_a85`):\n  `<~..~>` -> `a85decode(t, adobe=True)`, else `a85decode` (z), `b85decode`, Z85 (translate to b85's alphabet); sniff\n  magic, UTF-8 + slug gate; Mastery via `register_free(\"a85\", 23, fn)` on the RAW line (Pitfalls, D23).\n- **Framing / length prefix (D24)** -- single/TT = ONE line of netstrings `<len>:<data>,`, no separators/newline, EVERY\n  len = `1` (444 B, 4 B/char); the payload's own `,` `:` and spaces are data (`1:,,`, `1::,`, `1: ,`). Mastery line 24\n  = the same over the fragment (`1:o,1:n,1:-,`), 810/810 prefixes `1`. Parse rule (`_netstr`): cursor loop -- `\\s*(\\d+):`\n  at i, take EXACTLY `len` chars, require `,` (or end), advance; WHOLE text must parse (else None); never `split(',')`.\n  Covers len > 1/mixed; tnetstrings/bencode/RESP/chunked/varint/indexed/RLE frames are prebuilt siblings.\n- **Esoteric program + its SPELLING (D26-D27)** -- the blob is a PROGRAM; its output is the text. Program unchanged:\n  ONE cell from 0, each char = a run of ONLY `+` or ONLY `-` = delta from the previous char, then `.`; no loops/`<>`/`,`.\n  Spelling changed: D26 raw BF `+ - .` (~31 B/char; Mastery line 26 = 166-217 B); D27 Ook! `Ook.`/`Ook!` single-spaced,\n  PAIRED from token 0, only `..` + / `!!` - / `!.` output, NO `Ook?` (~314 B/char; line 27 = 1672-2172 B, cell 0 again).\n  Parse rule: tokenize the WHOLE text into dialect tokens (leftover must be empty), map pairs to BF, step-capped `_bf`\n  (8-bit cells, `,` = 0), printable + slug gate. lib `bf_dec`/`bfd_dec` (Ook/Blub/COW/Pikalang/... + crib-solved\n  unknown map); GM-faithful encoders `bf_enc` / `bfd_enc(s, \"ook\")`.\n- **Line wrapper / positional routing** -- `NN<TAB>fragment`; fragments split mid-word, join verbatim in number order.\n  `decode._positional` decodes line k with day k's REGISTERED codec; a DAYS entry must round-trip (`enc(dec(line)) ==\n  line`). Unregistered or non-round-tripping day -> `_unknown_line_cands` (re-\"decodes\" plain text, D25 `le-52` -> `-07`)\n  -> joint search (D26 absolute `'+'*ord+'.>'` vs GM deltas: 64.9 s kill; D27 same in Ook: 34.9 s). D28 had NO DAYS[28]\n  and passed only because line 28 was LAST. Rule: every new day = `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line, or a\n  DAYS entry with lib's GM-faithful encoder; assert the round trip on one real Mastery line.\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 83 x 17 on day 17, ..., 108 x 22, ..., 128 x 26, 133 x 27, 138 x 28 on day 28); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, but `fromhex` is junk (`#S\\xd2G...`) and the text ENDS in `...6e4`/`...e4` (prompt \"backwards\", \"back\", \"reversed\" + \"sixteen\") | reversed hex (D28): `python3 -c \"import sys;print(bytes.fromhex(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip()[::-1]).decode())\" blob.txt`; any strict charset that refuses to decode -> undo rev/rot13/Atbash first (`decode_chunk` does rev+hex) |\n| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` framed too (`1:,,`, `1::,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: loop `m=re.compile(r'(\\d+):').match(t,i); k=int(m[1]); j=m.end(); out.append(t[j:j+k]); assert t[j+k] == ','; i=j+k+1` (D24 solver `_netstr`). Siblings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag = tnetstring, `$5\\r\\n` RESP, hex-size lines = HTTP chunked, `N:c` with N > len = index or run count |\n| `U+004E U+0069 U+0063 ...` -- only `U+` + 4 hex tokens, one space apart, ~7 B per char (prompt \"point\", \"code point\", \"every character\") | code points (D25): `python3 -c \"import re,sys;print(''.join(chr(int(h,16)) for h in re.findall(r'[Uu]\\+([0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8})',open(sys.argv[1]).read())))\" blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.uplus_dec` (also `\\U0001F600`, `%u`, `0x`). Names (`LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N`) -> `unicodedata.lookup` |\n| Only `+ - .` (or `+-<>[].,`), runs of one sign then `.`, ~31 B per char, no letters (prompt \"language\", \"small\", \"brain\", \"food for thought\", \"say\") | Brainfuck (D26), loop-free one-cell form: `python3 -c \"import sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read();print(''.join(chr((t[:i].count('+')-t[:i].count('-'))%256) for i,c in enumerate(t) if c=='.'))\" blob.txt`; anything with `<>[]` -> `lib.warmups_codecs.bf_dec`. NEVER run untrusted programs outside a step-capped pure-python interpreter |\n| `Ook. Ook. Ook! Ook! ...` -- 2-3 word+mark tokens, ~314 B per char (prompt quotes the tokens, \"chatty\", \"monkey\", \"ape\", \"librarian\"); `Blub.`, `MoO mOo`, `pi ka pikachu`, any <= 8 repeating words with run structure | BF token dialect (D27 Ook!): `python3 -I -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.bfd_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (Ook/Blub/COW/Pikalang/Alphuck/ReverseFuck/Binaryfuck + crib-solved unknown map). Hand: tokens `re.findall(r'Ook([.?!])')`, pair from 0, `Ook! Ook.` = OUTPUT. Sanity: number of output pairs == sentence length (~108-110) |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\nPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\nrot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\nWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\nrefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n\n**Days 14-27** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n+ `fffe`; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff`; gzip in base64; zlib `78da` in base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`; netstrings\n`1:N,1:i,...`; `U+XXXX` code points; loop-free one-cell Brainfuck; the same program in Ook! pairs): single CORRECT in\n4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons (details in Pitfalls): D14 rank 3-word slugs first; D15 ROT47 needs\n`register_free`; D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer; D17 Mastery needed an early return; D18 lib's\n`rail_solve` existed but the carried solver never routed to it; D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 and D21's\nmagic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 unchanged (write the GENERIC rule on day N); D21 Mastery died at 60 s on an ad-hoc\nsolver; D23 and D25 Mastery all-NN-wrong from a pre-decoded line (fix: `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line);\nD24 a strict whole-text cursor parser sits first -- a structural codec's repeating unit (`\\d+:.,`) IS the cue. D26\n(`+ - .` soup; atlas had rated BF LOW): worker wrote a step-capped `_bf` (~27 s); Mastery died at 60 s because the\nruntime DAYS[26] encoder (`'+'*ord(c)+'.>'`) didn't reproduce the GM's DELTA bytes. D27 (\"Ook. Ook? Ook! Someone seems\nchatty.\"/\"Monkey Business\"): 33929 B, 3393 Ook pairs, only `+ - .` pairs, no `Ook?` despite the prompt; lib's prebuilt\n`bfd_dec` covered it, single ~33 s; the solver's DAYS[27] Ook encoder was absolute again (Mastery solve 34.9 s of 60).\nA parallel live worker swapped `Ook! Ook.` (output) / `Ook. Ook!` (input). A program blob means INTERPRET (pure python,\nstep cap); when lib has a prebuilt decoder for the family, call it first and register ITS encoder.\n\n**Day 28: Back in Sixteen** (\"Our sixteen-fingered friend is walking backwards.\" -- REVERSED HEX).\n- 220-byte `blob.txt`, one line, `[0-9a-e]` only, even length, no newline: D3's hex of the sentence, the STRING reversed\n  char by char (`...6e4` at the end = `4e` \"N\" read backwards). Pun: D3's own riddle (\"sixteen fingers\" = hex) +\n  \"walking backwards\" = D12 reversal; title \"Back in Sixteen\" (Back in Black) = back(wards) + sixteen. Triage again said\n  \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) and its idea list said only \"hex\" -- the second clue came from the prompt verb.\n- Command: `python3 -c \"import sys;print(bytes.fromhex(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip()[::-1]).decode())\" blob.txt`.\n  Forward `fromhex` gives `#S\\xd2GVvveu...` and byte-reversed gives `\\xe4\\x966V\\x02w...` (nibble-swapped) -- neither is\n  a slug; reverse the CHARACTERS (or byte-reverse + nibble-swap).\n- Single: the harness race probe (prebuilt lib `decode_chunk` reversal + hex / tgt:free-over-strict) submitted CORRECT at\n  start+8 s, first try; slug as printed. TT 5.1 s, Mastery 138 x 28 9.3 s (solve 6.2 s), both first try. The carried\n  solver registered NO day-28 codec: line 28 was the LAST Mastery line, so `_positional`'s unknown-last-line path\n  (`decode_chunk`: reversal then hex) decoded it. Tomorrow line 28 is a MIDDLE line -- register it (Pitfalls).\n- Lesson: after a 2-day esolang run the lane went back to COMPOSING two early codecs, cued by quoting an old prompt;\n  expect more remixes (reversed octal/binary/base64, rot13 over base64, ...) -- undo the outer free layer, then peel.\n\n## GM answer conventions learned\n- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-28; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n\n## Pitfalls that cost time\n- DELIVERY: Mastery = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt` (check magic, not extension); read member `blob-0N.txt`, never the\n  bundle (D7: a slug that is a word suffix `py-...` is the tell). Missing set bytes = a downloader problem (D1, ~12 fails),\n  not a solver one. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the real layout: test on the raw delivery.\n- NEVER REPLACE THE LANE SOLVER (D21): copy the previous day's warmups `solve.py` and add the new codec as ONE branch\n  (whole-blob step + `register_free(name, day, fn)`). A fresh generic brute-force solver passes single/TT (one short\n  blob) and then dies at 60 s on Mastery (103 sets x 21 lines). A prebuilt lib codec is equally useless if the carried\n  solver never routes to it (D18 `rail_solve`). First move on a new day: lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` +\n  `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on\n  a miss (83 x 17 passed 60 s when every set paid the joint search; a per-set `peel` BFS blew a 30 s window on D12). Time\n  the real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new codec; profile `answer()` per set when solve passes ~5 s.\n- PRE-DECODING / MIS-REGISTERED MASTERY LINES (D23 113/113, D25 123/123 sets wrong once; D26 60 s kill; D27 34.9 s):\n  never rewrite a numbered line to plaintext before `decode_chunked`/`_joint_free` (no \"identity\" choice: ROT5 turned\n  `el-46` into `el-91`); register today's codec on the RAW line even if the whole-blob path decodes it; a DAYS encoder\n  that doesn't reproduce the GM bytes is worse than none (see Variant axes: line wrapper). All sets wrong in the same\n  field = one line's routing. D28: no day-28 codec, saved only by being the LAST line -> register `(28, \"revhex\",\n  lambda s: s.encode().hex()[::-1], lambda t: bytes.fromhex(t[::-1]).decode(), False)` before line 28 is a middle line.\n- ENGLISH SCORING: don't count single letters/hex reads as English (D3 timeout); a rot-N must beat the unshifted text by a\n  margin (D4). Rot-N is `decode_chunk`'s catch-all, so ANY unknown plain transform comes back as plausible rot garbage\n  (D12 reversal `69-fove-n`, D13's rejected `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31` = rot-N of Atbash): gibberish slug words = an unknown\n  transform, not a GM quirk. Several free transforms in one blob (rot13/rev/Atbash/Caesar/ROT47) -> decide JOINTLY, each\n  once (`_joint_free`), rank full 3-word slugs first (D14 set22); prune by alphabet before the joint search (D15).\n- REVERSAL GRANULARITY (D28): a reversed STRICT string is still in the strict charset, so it \"decodes\" to junk instead of\n  failing -- `fromhex` succeeded on D28's reversed hex. Junk from a strict decode = try the outer free transforms\n  (char-reverse, byte-reverse + nibble-swap, rot13/Atbash of the letters) BEFORE assuming a new codec.\n- CLASS CONFUSION: binary is also hex (try binary first); decimal is also octal (padding + case); hex is a base64 subset\n  (triage says \"looks base64\" on D19-D20 hex); unpadded base64 must be re-padded (`len%4==1` is the only impossible length).\n- FRAGMENTS: split mid-word; join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace (never `.strip()` a decoded fragment; spaces are\n  ciphertext in transpositions). No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Regex-check the slug.\n- FRAMED / LENGTH-PREFIXED text (D24 netstrings): the payload contains the delimiters (`1:,,`, `1::,`), so `split(',')`\n  / `split(':')` corrupts exactly the punctuation the crib needs. Parse by COUNT with a cursor and demand the WHOLE text\n  parse (strict -> None) so the step can run first without stealing other blobs. `count:char` (RLE) and `len:data`\n  (netstring) read identically while every count is 1 -- a frame with N > 1 decides which.\n- STACKED LAYERS: a decode whose output is only hex/digits/`a`-`p`/16 symbols is a MIDDLE layer (D16 Morse -> hex, D17\n  Bacon -> nibbles): decode again. Two prompt cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\"; \"wrapper\" + \"compressed\") = two layers.\n- COMPRESSED layers (D21): the decoded bytes are binary, so printable/English gates reject the RIGHT branch; sniff the\n  magic bytes after every base-N/hex decode and decompress before scoring. Use `zlib.decompressobj(31)` / `wbits=-15`\n  when a gzip/deflate stream is truncated or headerless (`gzip.decompress` raises on a missing CRC trailer). `gzip -dc`\n  rejects zlib (`78xx`) input; detect zlib by the header checksum `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0`, not only `0x78` (D22).\n- BYTE-LEVEL layers (D19-D20 UTF-16 under hex): UTF-8-only or latin-1 hex decoders give `ÿþN\\x00i\\x00...`, never a slug.\n  BOM (`fffe`, `feff`, `efbbbf`, `fffe0000`) or NUL rhythm -> the matching Unicode codec, UTF-32 before UTF-16; BOM-less\n  `utf-16` uses the platform order, so name `-le`/`-be`. Never hard-code a day's byte order (Mastery holds both).\n- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18): wrong rail offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug; accept only a read that\n  STARTS `Nice work` and ends in a full slug. Mastery fragments restart the key PER LINE (6-8 chars) -- only the joined\n  slug gate confirms them; `decode_chunked` alone leaves line 18 scrambled (D21 duel: `Master:i rbsk-...`).\n- TOOLING: `str.replace`/sed solver patches silently no-op on a mismatched anchor (D8) -- assert the count. A literal\n  backslash-u typed into a Bash/heredoc reached python already unescaped -- test on the artifact or `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n  Two Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` drops `!` `,` `:` `-`; use `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec`.\n- PROGRAM blobs (D26 Brainfuck): interpret in a pure-python, step-capped interpreter (8-bit wrapping cells, `,` = 0);\n  never hand them to node/eval/an online runner. A loop-free one-cell BF is just a running sum of `+`/`-` read at `.`.\n  A BF charset test also matches Morse (`.`/`-`/space, D16 line 16): gate the interpreter's OUTPUT (printable + slug),\n  never the charset alone. Token dialects (D27 Ook): tokenize THEN pair from token 0 -- never substring-count\n  `'Ook! Ook.'` on the raw text (matches straddle pair boundaries). `Ook! Ook.` = output `.`, `Ook. Ook!` = input `,`;\n  a swapped table prints nothing or junk -- the output-pair count must equal the sentence length.\n- A TT start before a verified solver burns the clock (D18, 30 s). Timed windows leave no room for an LLM.\n- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n","numLines":254,"startLine":1,"totalLines":254}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KBWGoQ469bwmSXSiHygiUS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
